**Success Criteria

The project will be considered successful when the website clearly explains the Movement to Work programme and helps users understand how taking part could support their employment journey.

**User Understanding

**Users should be able to quickly understand:

*What Movement to Work is
*Who the programme is for
*How long the programme lasts
*Which days participants will attend
*What participants will do
*Which skills participants may develop
*What support is available
*How the programme could improve their CV
*How to register interest or find out more

**User Experience

**The website should:

*Use clear and supportive language
*Have a simple and predictable navigation structure
*Work effectively on mobile, tablet and desktop devices
*Present important information in short, manageable sections
*Make the main call to action easy to find
*Meet appropriate accessibility and colour-contrast standards
*Allow users to navigate the website using a keyboard
*Use readable text sizes and clear headings

**Programme Communication

**The website should successfully communicate:

*The two-week programme structure
*The six-day Monday-to-Wednesday schedule
*The approximate 10:00am to 3:00pm attendance time
*The practical work-experience activities
*The employability support available
*The transferable skills participants can develop
*The certificate-of-participation benefit
*The availability of one-to-one support
*Relevant local sectors and travel considerations

**Measurable Success Indicators

**Where possible, success will be measured through:

*Positive feedback from Jobcentre staff
*Positive feedback from potential participants
*Users successfully finding key programme information
*Users completing key tasks during usability testing
*An increase in expressions of interest or programme enquiries
*Reduced need for staff to repeatedly explain basic programme details
*Strong mobile usability and accessibility audit results
*Clear calls to action that direct users towards the next step
*Content approval from the relevant Jobcentre representative

The final website should give potential participants the confidence and information needed to decide whether Movement to Work is the right next step for them.
